From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Deividas Nastajus <16858119+dns54@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:17:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] mb/dell/sklkbl_desktops: Report errors, alerts and
 intrusion as Dell does

A headless machine that stops in firmware, or boots with a problem,
gives no hint why. Report it the way Dell's OptiPlexes of this
generation do.

Diagnostic codes: implement die_notify(). Blink Dell's code on the
power LED in two groups (Dell KB 000126021) through the SCH5555 LED
register until power-off, and play the 1-3-2 memory beep code for a
memory error. Codes: 3,3 no memory (no SPD found), 2,7 memory failure
(any romstage error, FSP-M's memory training first among them), 3,6
system board failure (any other stage). The memory code always beeps,
whatever the speaker and Beep Codes options say: Dell keeps that one
beep code and has no option to silence it.

Chassis intrusion: the case switch drives the PCH's INTRUDER# pin,
which latches INTRD_DET in TCO2_STS, in the RTC well, so it records an
opened case even while the system is off. coreboot leaves it set when
INTRUDER# isn't routed to SMI (bootleg's defconfig turns that SMI off).
Add Dell's "Chassis Intrusion" (Disabled / Enabled / On-Silent, default
Enabled) and "Clear Intrusion Warning" options. While an intrusion is
latched and the option isn't Disabled, measure it into PCR 1, so
secrets sealed to PCR 1 don't unseal. With Enabled, it is also shown as
a POST alert. Clear Intrusion Warning clears the latch on the next
boot. This runs at BS_DEV_INIT_CHIPS, before the SPI lockdown blocks
the option writes from outside SMM.

Removing the coin cell clears the latch with the rest of the RTC well.
So while the option isn't Disabled, also measure a loss of RTC power
(RTC_BATTERY_DEAD, which coreboot reports on one boot) into PCR 1. A
battery change then changes PCR 1 once too.

POST alerts: pass the problems coreboot finds before the payload in a
hidden, volatile CFR number, boot_alerts: a chassis intrusion with
"Chassis Intrusion" Enabled, a dead RTC battery, a thermal trip on the
previous boot, and a fan that the SCH5555 monitors stopped or below its
minimum speed (sch555x_fan_failure(), Dell's "Fan failure"). The
payload shows them at boot without stopping it.

Beep Codes, shown while the internal speaker is on: Disabled, Errors
(two short beeps from the payload with an alert, the default) or Errors
and POST (also one short beep when POST completes without an alert).

All beeps come from the 8254's counter 2, as on Dell, at Dasharo's
pitches; the memory code is 800 Hz, 300 ms. The ALC3234 comes out of
reset with its PC beep input, mixers and speaker pin muted: open that
path to the internal speaker and turn on its EAPD. The memory code
beeps in romstage, before ramstage initializes the codec, so open the
path there too: bring the HDA link out of reset at a temporary BAR
first, and configure the PCH's SPKR pad (GPP_B14) in the bootblock.
The GPIO table that selects it only runs in ramstage, and until then
the 8254's output never reached the codec.

A warm reboot doesn't reset the codec, and settings that Linux's
Realtek driver leaves behind keep the beep off the speaker even with
that path open. Start the 3050's verb table with AZALIA_RESET, as many
boards do, so every boot starts from the codec's defaults.

FSP gates the 8254's clock unless "Legacy 8254 Timer" is on, which
turns a beep into a click. So the menu leaves that option out, and the
board builds with USE_LEGACY_8254_TIMER (bootleg's defconfig): the 8254
always runs, as on Dell's firmware (which has no such option) and
Dasharo's boards.

Signed-off-by: Deividas Nastajus <16858119+dns54@users.noreply.github.com>
